#
# Line endings are a determinism concern here, not a style one. Git for Windows rewrites
# LF to CRLF on checkout by default, and a golden file or a per-tick hash chain that comes
# back with different bytes than it went in with fails a byte comparison on Windows only
# (docs/spikes/G4-determinism.md, section 5, "Line endings and path handling").

# Normalise text in the repository, check it out with LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that are byte-compared: never touched, on any platform.
tests/golden/** -text
*.hashes.txt -text
# Traces that are byte-compared across operating systems in the same way and are
# force-added by hand when a mismatch has to be documented (docs/spikes/G4-determinism.md
# sections 4 and 5). Without this pattern the blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` above would
# normalise exactly the file whose bytes are the finding.
*.trace.txt -text
*.binpb -text -diff
*.vox -text -diff
*.png -text -diff
*.ogg -text -diff
*.wav -text -diff

# Scripts that must stay executable-shaped with LF endings, including on Windows, because
# Git Bash and the CI runners execute them directly.
*.sh text eol=lf
scripts/* text eol=lf

# Windows-only files keep CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Generated or vendored trees that should not clutter a diff or a language breakdown.
Cargo.lock -diff linguist-generated
docs/spec/*.html linguist-documentation
